{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>וַיַּרְא בָּלָק בֶּן צִפּוֹר… וַיָּגָר מוֹאָב…</b> One needs to know for what matter the verse suspended the seeing in Balak and not in the masters of the dread, who are Moab. Second, why did only Moab fear and not Midian — and all the more so since the Midianites were the ones for whom fear was fitting, for they were destroyed and not Moab, and their astrological fortune would foresee whatever would befall them." "It appears according to their words (Numbers Rabbah 20:4) that they said Balak was one of the princes of Midian, but because of the dread of Israel they made him king of Moab over them. From this point, in the statement ״וַיַּרְא בָּלָק״ the verse disclosed the dread and fear of Midian, for at that time, in that situation, he was the great prince of Midian. And although it did not explicitly mention the fear, it is understood through the mention of Israel's deed against the Amorite — that they are the force overwhelming all inhabitants of the land. And afterward it mentioned the dread of Moab as well, as it is written ״וַיָּגָר מוֹאָב״, and from the convergence of the two fears they made peace between themselves: ״וַיֹּאמֶר מוֹאָב לְזִקְנֵי״ etc. And so they said, of blessed memory (ibid.), in this language: ״Now were not the Moabites fighting with the Midianites, as it is written: ״הַמַּכֶּה אֶת מִדְיָן בִּשְׂדֵה מוֹאָב״ (Genesis 36:35), and there was hatred between them from of old — a parable of two dogs״ etc., end quote. Behold that both of them feared. And the reason for the subjugation of Moab, that they accepted a prince of Midian as king over them and not the reverse, is because Moab needed their counsel, since Moses grew up among them, as they said of blessed memory (ibid.), and therefore they submitted to them and crowned their prince." "<b>Further,</b> it merits attention yet another point: why it used the language of seeing and not the language of hearing. And although our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Mekhilta) in this language: ״There is seeing that is hearing, such as ״וְכָל הָעָם רוֹאִים אֶת הַקּוֹלוֹת״״ end quote — elsewhere (Mekhilta) we find that they interpreted that they literally saw the sounds. Further, why was it necessary to mention his father's name ״בֶּן צִפּוֹר״? Further, why did it precision to say two inclusios <b>אֵת כָּל</b>? Further, one needs to know why it repeated to say <b>וַיָּגָר מוֹאָב</b> etc., <b>וַיָּקָץ</b> etc. Further, why once did it call them ״הָעָם״ and once call them ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״?" "This will be explained according to the way of their words of blessed memory (Zohar Chadash here) that Balak was a greater sorcerer than Balaam, and he would perform his sorceries through a certain bird whose name was well known — and that is what it means by <b>וַיַּרְא בָּלָק</b>: and what did he see? In the bird. And that is what it means by <b>בֶּן צִפּוֹר</b> — meaning, by virtue of his being a bird-son, he saw through the sorceries of the bird. And it used the word <b>בֶּן</b> because the bird was to him as father and captain, and through it he knew all that Israel had done to the Amorite, with no need for informants to make known matter small or great." "<b>And it is possible</b> that the verse's intent is that before the event he saw in the bird, and it precision to say <b>אֵת כָּל</b>: the inclusion of ״כָּל״ alludes to its body — that he saw in the bird all that Israel had done to the Amorite; and the inclusion of ״את״ alludes to another seeing alongside it — namely a nation that was destined to experience the like, namely the Amorite nation that Israel is destined to annihilate — and which nation is it that Israel uprooted then? It is Midian. And this is alluded to in the word <b>אֵת</b>. Here you learn that he saw what they did to the Amorite and what they would do to Midian. And it is possible that he saw as well that Moab is assuredly safe from the hostility of Israel, as indeed was the case, for sorcery tells the future whether for evil or for good. From this point the verse suspended the seeing in Balak, for he alone is the one who sees in the bird; and this seeing was seen by no one but Balak through his sorceries. And the intent of the narrative in this matter is to preface the causes that brought about the matter of Balaam's summons and what ensued from it in sins for Israel — that Balak's seeing was one cause among the causal complex, as I shall explain. And further, <b>וַיָּגָר מוֹאָב</b> is not a declaration of what followed from Balak's report, but a declaration of another cause for the event that followed — that entered Moab's heart as dread." "<b>And it was precise</b> to say <b>because of the people</b> — meaning, on account of their being numerous and mighty, as it says ״כִּי רַב הוּא״. And it added to say <b>וַיָּקָץ</b> etc. — it intends to say that even though dread had entered their hearts, despite all this they would have found a remedy for the matter in a natural way, by hiring many and strong peoples against them. Rather, their trouble was trouble specifically because they were Israel, whose victory is not according to nature but by miracle and wonder — and that is what it says, ״וַיָּקֻצוּ <b>מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל</b>״ — meaning, their being children of Israel. And in the midst of all this, Moab made peace with Midian, and Balak became king over them, and they sent for Balaam — and his outcomes were a court of death." "And if any one of all the aforementioned causes were absent, they would not have sent for Balaam and nothing would have come of it. For if Balak had not had insight into hidden matters, he would not have grasped to know what would befall him and his people together with Midian, and he would not have consented to make peace with Moab to ally with them against Israel. And even if he had sent for Balaam while he was still in his place in Midian, the matter of the licentiousness of the women whom the Moabites made freely available would not have come about — for Midian was not assured regarding Israel's coming against them as the Moabites were, because he [Balaam] had told them in his prophecy that they would do no harm and cause no destruction to them until the end of days, as it is written (Numbers 24:14) ״אֵת אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה הָעָם הַזֶּה לְעַמְּךָ בְּאַחֲרִית הַיָּמִים״, and from this the Moabites were assured to send their daughters to the camp of the Hebrews and to engage with them (Sanhedrin 106a), for they were at peace with them — unlike Midian, of whom we do not find that they made freely available any but a single Midianite woman, as they of blessed memory said (Midrash Rabbah here) that she was the daughter of Balak, for he is the one called ״צוּר״. Also, if the dread of Moab had not been upon them from the people, even though they were disgusted because of the children of Israel as we explained the reason for the doubling etc., they would not have made peace with Midian and appointed princes over them. Therefore HaShem preceded the cause and said <b>וַיַּרְא בָּלָק</b> etc., <b>וַיָּגָר</b> etc., <b>וַיָּקָץ</b> etc. — and this was the cause of <b>וַיֹּאמֶר מוֹאָב</b> etc., and ״וַיִּשְׁלַח מַלְאָכִים״ etc., and what came of it came of it."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {4 "Bracketed אוּמָּה supplied by editor; translated as 'namely a nation' following the flow of the argument. The printed אוֹמָר (saying) is hyper-literally rendered. Textual difficulty recorded.", 6 "The reading ״שֶׁהִיא בִּתּוֹ שֶׁל בָּלָק״ follows the plain sense of the text as transmitted; the referent of ״he is the one called צוּר״ is uncertain but rendered literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:30.353927656Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}